# Old Squarespace URLs -> this site. Read by Cloudflare Pages at deploy time.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The church's live sitemap has 302 URLs. Ten are real
# pages and 292 are podcast episodes, and every one of them is indexed by
# Google today and printed in whatever links people have saved. Six of the ten
# real pages do not exist here under the same path, so without this file the
# cutover turns the church's entire search presence into 404s on the day the
# nameservers move.
#
# MAPPING RULE. Each old URL goes to the page that now does its job, not to
# the home page. A redirect to "/" is what you write when you have not read the
# old page; Google treats a mass redirect to the root as a soft 404 and drops
# the URL anyway, so it buys nothing and costs the visitor their place.
#
# What each old page actually was, checked against the live site on 2026-07-27:
#
#   /home                    Squarespace's duplicate of the home page. 200s today.
#   /contact-us              Service times, street address, phone, email. That is
#                            /visit now, and it was built from this page.
#   /what-we-believe         The Church of God declaration of faith. This URL is
#                            now a real page again, so it needs NO redirect rule;
#                            the old address resolves to the new page directly.
#   /statement-of-faith      The same material, second URL. -> /what-we-believe.
#   /staff                   "Meet the team" — Brad, Caleb, Daniel and others.
#                            /about carries the same people under "Who leads".
#   /knowing-christ          A gospel page: who Jesus is and an invitation to
#                            respond. That page now exists again at the same URL
#                            (built 2026-08-21), so this needs NO redirect rule
#                            either. The note that used to sit here said "if a
#                            page like this gets written, point this rule at it";
#                            what happened instead is better, because the old
#                            address is the new page.
#   /media                   "Browse the sermon archive" plus podcast links. /watch.
#   /sermon-video            Recorded services, incl. children's church. /watch.
#   /mobile-app              How to install Church Center. /give is where this
#                            site covers that app (way 02), so it goes there
#                            rather than to /watch.
#   /give                    Same path here. No rule needed, and adding one would
#                            create a redirect to itself.
#
# TRAILING SLASHES, both ends of the rule.
#
# On the left: Cloudflare Pages matches the request path exactly, so
# /contact-us and /contact-us/ are two different matches. Every rule is written
# twice for that reason. The /* form also catches anything that was nested
# under the old path.
#
# On the right: the destinations carry a trailing slash because Pages serves
# these as directory indexes and 308s /visit to /visit/ on its own. Writing the
# bare path costs a legacy visitor two redirects (this 301, then that 308) for
# no reason. Verified against `wrangler pages dev dist`, which runs the real
# rule engine locally.
#
# Rule budget: Pages allows 2,100 static and 100 dynamic (wildcard) rules.
# This file uses 11 of the dynamic budget.

/home                                /             301
/home/*                              /             301

/contact-us                          /visit/        301
/contact-us/*                        /visit/        301

# /what-we-believe is a REAL PAGE now, not a redirect. Do not re-add a rule for
# it: a redirect here would shadow the page and send its own URL to /about.
/statement-of-faith                  /what-we-believe/    301
/statement-of-faith/*                /what-we-believe/    301
/staff                               /about/        301
/staff/*                             /about/        301
# /knowing-christ is a REAL PAGE now. Same warning as above: no rule.

/media                               /watch/        301
/media/*                             /watch/        301
/sermon-video                        /watch/        301
/sermon-video/*                      /watch/        301

/mobile-app                          /give/         301
/mobile-app/*                        /give/         301

# The podcast. 292 URLs, one per episode plus the index. They all become /watch,
# which is the archive those episodes now live in and plays every one of them.
/brookside-main-worship-podcast      /watch/        301
/brookside-main-worship-podcast/*    /watch/        301

# NOT HANDLED HERE, deliberately:
#
#   www -> apex. Pages serves whatever custom domains are attached to the
#   project, so attaching both would serve the same site on two hostnames and
#   split it in Google's index. The redirect belongs at the zone level as a
#   Cloudflare Redirect Rule, because it has to fire for every hostname on the
#   zone rather than only for requests that reach this project. See
#   dns-cutover-runbook.md in the Compass repo.
#
#   brooksidecog.com -> brooksidechurchofgod.com. Squarespace does this today
#   (301, verified). Same reason: zone-level rule on the brooksidecog.com zone.
#
#   /s/* Squarespace asset URLs. Those are images and files served off
#   Squarespace's own CDN, not paths on this domain, so they die with the
#   account no matter what this file says.
